= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use <unitary gauge> and write $\phi=v+h/\sqrt2$. The quadratic Lagrangian contains
$$
\frac12(\partial h)^2-\lambda v^2h^2+e^2v^2A_\mu A^\mu.
$$
Thus the radial Higgs excitation is a spin-zero particle with $m_h^2=2\lambda v^2$, while the gauge boson is a massive spin-one particle with $m_A^2=2e^2v^2$. The phase of $\phi$ is the <Goldstone boson> eaten by the <Higgs mechanism> to supply the longitudinal polarization of $A_\mu$.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Varying $A_\nu$ gives the <Maxwell equations>
$$
\partial_\mu F^{\mu\nu}=J^\nu,
\qquad
J^\nu=ie\bigl[(D^\nu\phi)^\dagger\phi-\phi^\dagger D^\nu\phi\bigr].
$$
On the vacuum manifold, choose unitary gauge so that $\phi=v$. Then $\mathbf J=-2e^2v^2\mathbf A$ and the <London equation> is
$$
\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf J=-2e^2v^2\mathbf B.
$$
For a static configuration, $\boldsymbol\nabla\times\mathbf B=\mathbf J$ and $\boldsymbol\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0$, so
$$
\nabla^2\mathbf B=2e^2v^2\mathbf B=\frac1{\xi^2}\mathbf B,
\qquad
\boxed{\xi=\frac1{\sqrt2ev}=\frac1{m_A}}.
$$
The magnetic field therefore decays exponentially inside the condensate. This is the <Meissner effect>, with penetration depth equal to the inverse gauge-boson mass.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Writing $\Phi=(\phi_1,\phi_2)^T$, the scalar sector has a global $SU(2)$ flavour symmetry in addition to the gauged common $U(1)$ phase. A vacuum can be chosen as $\Phi_0=(0,v)^T$. A diagonal combination of the gauge phase and the $SU(2)$ rotation generated by $\sigma_3$ leaves it invariant.

The radial scalar has $m_h^2=2\lambda v^2$, the gauge boson has $m_A^2=2e^2v^2$, and two real scalars remain massless. The four real scalar directions consist of one radial mode and three angular modes; one angular mode is eaten, leaving the two <Goldstone boson>[Goldstone bosons] expected from the two broken physical global generators. The vacuum set before quotienting is $S^3$, and the manifold of gauge-inequivalent vacua is the <complex projective line>
$$
S^3/U(1)=\mathbb{CP}^1\cong S^2.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

